  "preamble": "Fronting of the voiced velar stop [ɡ] to the voiced palatal stop [ɟ] next to front vowels. It is a productive allophonic rule in Turkish native vocabulary and in Modern Greek, and it appears historically in Albanian, where Latin g before front vowels is reflected by gj [ɟ] in loanwords such as engjëll 'angel'.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Fronting",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Turkish",
      "languageFamily": "Turkic",
      "isAllophone": true,
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "gel 'come!' (/ɡ/ before a front vowel)",
          "to": "[ɟæl]",
          "note": "In native Turkic words the velars /k, ɡ/ are palatalized to [c, ɟ] when adjacent to the front vowels /e, i, œ, y/; the spelling does not mark the alternation."
        },
        {
          "from": "göz 'eye'",
          "to": "[ˈɟø̞z̪]"
        },
        {
          "from": "gece 'night'",
          "to": "[ɟe̞ˈd͡ʒɛ]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Modern Greek",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "isAllophone": true,
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "/ɡ/ (γγ) before /i/ in αγγίζω 'to touch'",
          "to": "[aŋˈɟi.zo]"
        },
        {
          "from": "/ɡ/ (γγ) before /e/ in άγγελος 'angel'",
          "to": "[ˈaɲ.ɟe.los]",
          "note": "Greek has palatals [c, ɟ, ç, ʝ] as allophones of the velars /k, ɡ, x, ɣ/ before the front vowels /e, i/."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Albanian",
      "languageFamily": "Albanian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Latin angelus 'angel' (g before e)",
          "to": "engjëll 'angel', with gj = [ɟ]",
          "note": "Borrowed through Vulgar Latin; the Albanian letter gj is the voiced palatal plosive [ɟ]."
        },
